  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Sam wants to grow as many apples in a one acre field as possible. He cannot simply plant as many trees as he can, as doing so reduces the yield of fruit each tree produces. From experience, Sam knows that if he plants 100 trees per acre, then each tree will yield 1100 apples. If he plants 200 trees per acre, then each tree will yield 600 apples. Assume that the yield per tree depends linearly on the number of trees planted per acre. How many trees should Sam plant in order to maximise his total yield of apples?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": {
        "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
        "version": 1,
        "blocks": [
          {
            "id": "p26-q12-short-result",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "160 trees.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p26-q12-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The total yield ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "Y=5x(320-x)",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " is maximised halfway between its intercepts.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $x$ be the number of trees planted and $A$ the number of apples per tree. Write $A=ax+b$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1100&=100a+b\\\\600&=200a+b\\\\500&=-100a\\\\a&=-5\\\\b&=1100-100(-5)\\\\b&=1600\\\\A&=1600-5x\\end{align*}$$\nThe total yield is\n$$\\begin{align*}Y&=x(1600-5x)\\\\Y&=5x(320-x).\\end{align*}$$\nThis downward-opening parabola has intercepts at $x=0$ and $x=320$. Its maximum occurs halfway between them:\n$$x=\\frac{0+320}{2}=160.$$\nSam should plant **160 trees** in his one acre field.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
